## Proof

Let $G$ be a finite group and $X = \{x : G \to \mathbb{C}\}$ the vector space of complex-valued functions on $G$. Define two representations $\rho_1, \rho_2 : G \to \mathrm{GL}(X)$ by
$$(\rho_1(g)x)(h) = x(g^{-1}h), \qquad (\rho_2(g)x)(h) = x(hg).$$

**These are representations.** For $\rho_1$:
$$(\rho_1(g_1)\rho_1(g_2)x)(h) = (\rho_1(g_2)x)(g_1^{-1}h) = x(g_2^{-1}g_1^{-1}h) = x((g_1g_2)^{-1}h) = (\rho_1(g_1g_2)x)(h).$$
For $\rho_2$:
$$(\rho_2(g_1)\rho_2(g_2)x)(h) = (\rho_2(g_2)x)(hg_1) = x(hg_1g_2) = (\rho_2(g_1g_2)x)(h).$$
So both are genuine left actions of $G$ on $X$. (These are the left and right regular representations of $G$ realized on functions.)

**The intertwining operator.** Define $T : X \to X$ by
$$(Tx)(h) := x(h^{-1}).$$
This is $\mathbb{C}$-linear, and it is invertible since $T^2 = \mathrm{id}$:
$$(T^2 x)(h) = (Tx)(h^{-1}) = x((h^{-1})^{-1}) = x(h).$$

**$T$ intertwines $\rho_1$ and $\rho_2$.** We verify $T\rho_1(g) = \rho_2(g)T$ for every $g \in G$. Evaluating both sides at an arbitrary $h \in G$:
$$\bigl(T(\rho_1(g)x)\bigr)(h) = (\rho_1(g)x)(h^{-1}) = x(g^{-1}h^{-1}) = x((hg)^{-1}),$$
$$\bigl(\rho_2(g)(Tx)\bigr)(h) = (Tx)(hg) = x((hg)^{-1}).$$
The two expressions coincide, so $T\rho_1(g) = \rho_2(g)T$ for all $g \in G$.

**Conclusion.** $T$ is a linear automorphism of $X$ satisfying $T \circ \rho_1(g) = \rho_2(g) \circ T$ for every $g \in G$. Hence $\rho_1$ and $\rho_2$ are equivalent representations: the inversion map $h \mapsto h^{-1}$ on $G$ induces the similarity between the left and right regular representations.

